ry lane. Virus Res . 2011;161(1):3–14. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 54. Labrique AB, Zaman K, Hossain Z, et al. Epidemiology and risk factors of incident hepatitis E virus infections in rural Bangladesh. Am J Epidemiol . 2010;172(8):952–961. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 55. Hall WT, Madden DL, Mundon FK, et al. Protective effect of immune serum globulin (ISG) against hepatitis A infection in a natural epidemic. Am J Epidemiol . 1977;106(1):72–75. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 56. Maynard JE. Passive immunization against hepatitis B: a review of recent studies and comment on current aspects of control. Am J Epidemiol . 1978;107(2):77–86. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 57. Lelie PN, Reesink HW, de Jong-van Manen ST, et al. Immunogenicity and safety of a plasma-derived heat-inactivated hepatitis B vaccine (CLB). Studies in volunteers at a low risk of infection with hepatitis B virus. Am J Epidemiol . 1984;120(5):694–702. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 58. Spicknall IH, Foxman B, Marrs CF, et al. A modeling framework for the evolution and spread of antibiotic resistance: literature review and model categorization. Am J Epidemiol . 2013;178(4):508–520. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 59. Xavier JB, Young VB, Skufca J, et al. The cancer microbiome: distinguishing direct and indirect effects requires a systemic view. Trends Cancer . 2020;6(3):192–204. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 60. Shrestha S, Foxman B, Weinberger DM, et al. Identifying the interaction between influenza and pneumococcal pneumonia using incidence data. Sci Transl Med . 2013;5(191):191ra84. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 61. Rowe HM, Meliopoulos VA, Iverson A, et al. Direct interactions with influenza promote bacterial adherence during respiratory infections. Nat Microbiol . 2019;4(8):1328–1336. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-