ork (NY): Springer Publishing Company; 2007. [ Google Scholar ] [17]. Piasecki J, Waligora M, Dranseika V. Google search as an additional source in systematic reviews. Sci Eng Ethics. 2018;24(2): 809–810. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [18]. Mezinska S, Kakuk P, Mijaljica G, et al. Research in disaster settings: a systematic qualitative review of ethical guidelines. BMC Med Ethics. 2016;17(1):62. doi: 10.1186/s12910-016-0148-7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [19]. Kahrass H, Borry P, Gastmans C, et al. PRISMA-ethics – reporting guideline for systematic reviews on ethics literature: development, explanations and examples. OSF Preprints. 2021. doi: 10.31219/osf.io/g5kfb. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] [20]. Barnhart A, Dierickx K. The many moral matters of organoid models: a systematic review of reasons. Med Health Care Philos. 2022; 25(3):545–560. doi: 10.1007/s11019-022-10082-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [21]. Aguilera B, Perez Gomez J, DeGrazia D. Should biomedical research with great apes be restricted? systematic review of reasons. BMC Med Ethics. 2021;22(1):15. doi: 10.1186/s12910-021-00580-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [22]. van Dijke I, Bosch L, Bredenoord A, et al. The ethics of clinical applications of germline genome modification: a systematic review of reasons. Hum Reprod. 2018;33(9):1777–1796. doi: 10.1093/humrep/dey257. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [23]. de Graeff N, Jongsma K, Johnston J, et al. The ethics of genome editing in non-human animals: a systematic review of reasons reported in the academic literature. Philos Trans R Soc Lond B Biol Sci. 2019;374(1772):20180106. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [24]. Mills M, Tropf F. Sociology, genetics, and the coming of age of sociogenomics. Annu Rev Sociol. 2020;46(1):553–581. doi: 10.1146/annurev-soc-121919-054756. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] [25]. Harden K, Koellinger P. Using genetics for social science. Nat Hum Behav. 2020;4(6):567–576. doi: 10.1038/s41562-020-0862-5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [26]. Machirori M, Patch C, Metcalfe A. “It didn’t mean anything”–moving within a landscape of knowledge to interpret genetics and genetic test results within familial cancer concerns. New Genet Soc. 2021;40(4):570–598. doi: 10.1080/14636778.2021.1997575. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] [27]. Raz A, Pontarotti G, Weitzman J. Epigenetic metaphors: an interdisciplinary translation of encoding and decoding. New Genet Soc. 2019;38(3):264–288. doi: 10.1080/14636778.2019.1601009. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] [28]. Moor J. Why we need better ethics for emerging technologies. Ethics Inf Technol. 2005;7(3):111–119. doi: 10.1007/s10676-006-0008-0. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Bioethics what Supp 2 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_2.docx (13.5KB, docx) Bioethics what Supp 1 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_1.pdf (82.9KB, pdf) Bioethics what Supp 3 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_3.docx (14KB, docx) Bioethics what Supp 4 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_4.docx (19KB, docx) Bioethics what Supp 5 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_5.docx (57.8KB, docx) Bioethics what Supp 7 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_7.docx (35.6KB, docx) Bioethics what Supp 6 NIHMS2040356-supplement-Bioethics_what_Supp_6.docx (693.1KB, docx)',15,'2025-10-20 06:02:44','2025-10-20 06:02:44');
