al Analogue Scale; HL = Hodgkin''s lymphoma; CNS = central nervous system tumor; OST = osteosarcoma; EWS = Ewing''s sarcoma; RBD = rhabdomyosarcoma; PRN = pro re nata (as needed); PeNAT = Pediatric Nausea Assessment Tool; CI = confidence interval; CINV = chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting; HD = Hodgkin''s disease; P6 = Pericardium 6; RINVR = Rhodes index of nausea, vomiting, and retching; QD = quaque die (once a day); D/C = discharge; MB = medulloblastoma; MANE = Morrow Assessment of Nausea and Antiemetics; AML = acute myeloid/myeloblastic leukemia; NV-VAS = Nausea and Vomiting Visual Analogue Scale; SDS = Symptom Distress Scale; ARINVc = Adapted Rhodes Index of Nausea and Vomiting for Pediatrics by Child Scale; ARINVp = Adapted Rhodes Index of Nausea and Vomiting for Pediatrics by Parent Scale; MAT = MASCC Antiemesis Tool; MASCC = Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer; VR = virtual reality; 5-HT 3 = 5-hydroxytryptamine 3 ; ES = effect size. a All participants received antiemetic therapy in combination with their assigned intervention or control condition. b The application of intervention in participant''s first chemotherapy session, previously chemotherapy-naive. Five studies reported the results of quasi-experimental studies; the remaining 13 studies reported the results of randomized controlled trials. Interventions were implemented for various lengths of time, depending on the participants’ type of chemotherapy and the associated protocol administration schedule. All studies used a 5HT 3 antagonist, the most used was ondansetron ( N = 18), and the next most used was granisetron ( n = 5). In addition to 5HT 3 antagonists, n = 10 studies reported use of additional antiemetics; the most common was dexamethasone ( n = 9), but others included medications like phenothiazines ( n = 3) and aprepitant ( n = 3). Six studies administered pharmacologic antiemetics according to chemotherapy/treatment protocols: two studies using single antiemetic protocols and four using multipleantiemetic protocols. Antiemetic use was consistent across control and intervention groups. Eight of the studies used control groups with only antiemetic medication, whereas the remaining 10 studies used antiemetic medication in combination with a sham/placebo intervention. CIM Therapy Characteristics Three CIM intervention categories were identified among included studies: herbal ( n = 4), physical ( n = 11), and psychological ( n = 4) therapies. Of note, one study implemented both an herbal and physical intervention, albeit used independently in two different intervention groups ( Essawy et al., 2021 ). The remaining 17 studies implemented only one intervention. The details of each included study organized by intervention category are reported in Table 2 . Herbal interventions included oral ginger (lozenges and capsules) and aromatherapy (essential oils). Ginger lozenges and capsules were dosed based on participant weight, while a predetermined number of essential oil drops were used for all participants. Physical interventions included the acupoint modalities of acupressure and acupuncture. Seven studies implemented Pericardium 6 (P6) acupressure; four via manual finger stimulation and three via wristband stimulation. Manual acupressure was performed by the researchers in two studies, compared to the performance by the participants after education in the other two studies. Two studies used SeaBands © and one study used BioBands © . Auricular acupressure was applied in one study via seed pressing (Yeh et al., 2012). Lastly, three studies implemented acupuncture: one solely with needles, another primarily via needle although laser acupuncture was an alternative if needles were not tolerated, and the remainder solely via laser acupuncture. The acupuncture points used were reported in only two of the three acupuncture studies and performed by experienced and specialized acupuncturists. Psychological interventions involved methods of distraction via virtual reality, mobile applications, and hypnotherapy. Virtual reality interventions involved the use of pediatric-appropriate videos, or cartoons, which participants were immersed in before and throughout the full length of chemotherapy administration. The mobile application featured music, puzzles, games, mandalas, and breathing exercises participants used only after chemotherapy was administered ( Semerci et al., 2022 ). Hypnotherapy, in the form of learned self-hypnosis, was used before, during, and after chemotherapy administration ( Hawkins et al., 1995 ). CINV Outcomes All but one study measured acute and/or delayed CINV. Anticipatory CINV was measured in two studies but was the sole outcome in only one of these studies ( Hawkins et al., 1995 ). Measurement scales with mixed objective and subjective measures, such as the Pediatric Nausea Assessment Tool (PeNAT) and the Rhodes Index of Nausea, Vomiting, and Retching (RINVR), were used in 13 studies. The remaining used subjective scales ( n = 2) to measure symptom distress or objective scales ( n = 3) to record the number of episodes of vomiting. Effects of Therapies on CINV Of the included studies, 10 found statistically significant results. This included three of the four herbal interventions: ginger lozenges and ginger capsules. The three studies concluded oral ginger when used in combination with pharmacological antiemetics decreased nausea and vomiting more than pharmacological antiemetics alone. However, ginger aromatherapy and pharmacological antiemetics showed no significant improvement over routine care. Of the physical interventions, four of the 10 studies found significant results. This included two of the four manually applied P6 acupressure studies. Yet, none of the three studies implementing P6 acupressure via wristband found significant results, nor did the study implementing auricular acupressure. The findings of studies examining acupuncture were mixed; two studies yielded statistically significant results while the other did not. Significant results were identified in one of two studies implementing needle acupressure as well as the study implementing only laser acupuncture. However, the study using both needle and laser acupuncture did not yield significant findings. Results among the psychological interventions were also mixed; two of four found significant results. The studies examining virtual reality interventions concluded this therapy was not effective, but two studies concluded an interactive mobile application and hypnotherapy were effective. Discussion In this scoping review, we aimed to review and summarize evidence on the combined effects of pharmacological antiemetics and CIM on CINV in pediatric patients with cancer. Despite the breadth of CIM modalities used as adjuncts to pharmacological antiemetics and included in this scoping review, there is no clear evidence of improved control of CINV. However, several patterns were observed in the analysis of the included studies. Among the herbal interventions, oral ginger was effective in reducing CINV compared to antiemetics alone, but the same effect was not observed for ginger aromatherapy. While effective results of ginger supplements were identified in some included studies in this review, the quality of evidence of such studies was low. Furthermore, the COG recommends against ginger use due to inconclusive results related to the herb''s therapeutic antiemetic effect in the adult population and a general lack of evidence in the pediatric population ( 2023 ). Ginger also poses an increased risk for bleeding due to potential anticoagulant effects as well as an increased risk of hypoglycemia due to effects on glucose clearance ( Daily et al., 2015 ; Quimby, 2007 ). Medication interactions, side effects, duration of use, and associated safety of herbal interventions should be carefully considered and discussed before integration into a patient''s treatment plan. Consideration of consultation with a trained Integrative Medicine provider is of value as these professionals can aid in assessing the benefits and risk profiles of CIM therapies from a safety-effectiveness evaluation lens ( Esparham et al., 2018 ). Additionally, manual acupressure was more effective in decreasing CINV when combined with pharmacological antiemetics than wristband acupressure, albeit one of the four studies investigating manual acupressure found no difference when compared to standard care. There were no differences in the results of the manual acupressure based on the method of application via participant or researcher. Discrepancies in the findings between manual and wristband acupressure leave the question of the accuracy of placement and whether the band adequately stimulates the P6 point. Additional research and clarification are needed to investigate this question, especially considering the ease of use and broad accessibility of acupressure wristbands. The findings of the acupuncture studies were inconclusive. The differences in findings could be due to the method of acupuncture (needle vs. laser), as well as variations in acupuncture points, size of acupoint needles, depth of needle insertion, and duration of needle placement. Acupuncture is reportedly safe to use in pediatric populations when implemented by appropriately trained professionals with rare and limited adverse effects ( NCI, 2023c ). However, substandard practice may lead to infection, nerve damage, pain, bruising, and bleeding ( NCI, 2023c ). There are no current recommendations from the COG on the implementation of acupuncture in their 2023 guidelines; however, their earlier 2021 guidelines suggested the therapy “may be effective” ( COG, 2021 , 2023 ). Patient safety should always be the foremost consideration before use. There were also no conclusive findings among the psychological interventions. Virtual reality used in combination with pharmacological antiemetics was not more effective than standard care of antiemetic medication alone. However, while virtual reality was not beneficial, other distraction interventions proved more promising as well as feasible, such as the interactive mobile application. While the virtual reality interventions required specialized, stationary setups within the institutions, the mobile app was easily downloaded onto patients’ tablets and/or phones, thus enabling better access and potentially wider applicability. Although no clear benefit was demonstrated for the use of CIM in addition to antiemetics, this finding is congruent with existing research in the adult oncology population. In a systematic review of combination treatment with complementary therapies and pharmacological antiemetics for CINV in the adult oncology population, Sickel et al. (2018) found similar inconclusive results. This study analyzed the same categories of interventions, including ginger, acupressure, acupuncture, and distraction such as guided imagery and music therapy, but found unclear, mixed results on the effectiveness of these therapies. The inconclusive results in the studies included in this review could be due to a variety of factors other than the ineffectiveness of the studied therapies. First, integrative medicine poses unique challenges to conducting research from the variability of complementary products to the safety of complementary-drug interactions and individual practitioner–patient beliefs about specific therapies; all potentially influencing results ( Curtis, 2004 ). Another explanation could be related to the method of outcome assessment. Only two studies used objective methods to evaluate CINV, while the remaining used subjective scales or scales with mixed components. Interestingly, the two studies which used objective measurement scales found significant findings. Additionally, a few measurement scales used to measure nausea and vomiting in included studies have not been validated in the pediatric population, including Edmonton''s Symptom Assessment Scale, MASCC Antiemesis Tool, and nonadapted RINVR ( Dupuis et al., 2012 ; Ewig et al., 2022 ). These scales are intended for adult use and pose an element of complexity too challenging for pediatric patients to effectively understand. Even when validated scales are used, children, especially young children, may be unable to understand or differentiate feelings of nausea and vomiting from other ailments, or accurately rate symptoms on a severity scale. There was also a lack of appropriate pharmacological antiemetic protocols utilized in the included studies. Six of the included studies adhered to antiemetic treatment protocols, but two of those used only single antiemetic therapies. Current guidelines recommend several different classes of antiemetics to address CINV. Thus, the lack of appropriate pharmacological antiemetic use could have contributed to insignificant findings and set a poor foundation to allow the complementary and integrative modalities to take their full therapeutic effect. Another important element is intervention duration. The length of interventions varied between 2 days and 4 weeks. A shorter intervention duration may not allow for the full effect of the complementary therapy to be observed. Along with duration, the timing during the patient''s treatment plan in which the intervention was implemented is also relevant. As previously discussed, poor control of nausea and vomiting during one chemotherapy cycle predicts poor control in subsequent cycles ( Dupuis & Nathan, 2012 ). Few studies included in this review limited participant inclusion to patients with no history of chemotherapy. The majority of included patients had a history of chemotherapy treatments and previous experiences of nausea and vomiting. If included patients already suffer from poor control, complementary therapies may be less likely to be effective than they would be in chemotherapy-naïve patients. Future studies should use an integrative approach at the beginning of a patient''s treatment, as integrative medicine is proactive and preventative at its core. This approach would enable a better understanding of whether prompt implementation of complementary therapies supports a greater therapeutic effect. Additionally, most of the included studies had small sample sizes and were inadequately powered to detect a true effect, if present. While the sample sizes were generally small, the samples were adequately composed of both genders and limited to the ages of children who could sufficiently self-report symptoms, therefore the results could be more generalizable to the broader pediatric oncology population. While 13 studies included in this review utilized a randomized controlled design, the remaining five implemented quasi-experimental designs, lacking randomization. This ultimately limits the ability to determine if the complementary and integrative intervention in use was the sole reason for their findings or if other uncontrolled factors contributed. The included studies in this study were not limited by publication date, thus the inclusion of sources spans 27 years (1995–2022). While literature searches are often limited to more recent years, the general lack of published evidence on CIM prompted the expanded time frame. Across this nearly 30-year time frame, CINV has persisted and remained a burden for pediatric patients and families. Despite changes in available pharmacological antiemetics, medications alone have not completely relieved symptom distress, leaving patients and families 30 years ago and now to seek alternative options. The field of CIM has increased in visibility and acceptance among pediatric patients, families, and providers providing this as a viable avenue for those seeking alternative options ( Esparham et al., 2018 ). The most prominent differences across the time frame of included studies are the recommendations and use of pharmacological antiemetics as well as changes in technology. There are few differences between the included studies in terms of study and sample characteristics. The CIM modalities are alike and similar in sample size, age range, sex distribution, and cancer diagnoses. There are also few differences in the quality and rigor of each study as all included studies in this scoping review are supported by weak evidence, as previously discussed. As for pharmacological antiemetics during this time frame, 5HT 3 antagonists, specifically ondansetron, remained a mainstay of treatment and was used consistently across the 30 years, along with dexamethasone. Three included studies in the early 2000s used phenothiazines to address CINV, but the use of this class of medications for nausea and vomiting slowly dissipated due to significant adverse effects, specifically extrapyramidal side effects ( Romano et al., 2019 ). The last included study to use phenothiazines was in 2008 ( Jones et al., 2018 ). However, another medication, (fos)aprepitant, was introduced as an option to address nausea and vomiting after the FDA approved the use of the medication in children in 2015 ( Romano et al., 2019 ). Three included studies used aprepitant, the first of which was published in 2018. Technology advancements between 1995 and 2022 have also been significant. Two included studies implemented virtual reality: one in 1995 and the other in 2022. Virtual reality evolved from CD-ROM and a headset in 1995 to a head-mounted device fitted to a smartphone in 2022, creating a more immersive experience. The development of smartphones and other devices also allowed for an interactive mobile application to address CINV. With these developments, technological interventions are more readily and easily accessible to patients and families. Limitations to this review are few. Although best efforts were made to expand and maximize search terms, including consultation and work with a research librarian, missing terms could have limited results and excluded additional sources with salient data. Similarly, though the four databases searched reflect a comprehensive look at published health sciences literature, studies not published in journals covered by the databases were potentially excluded. During the screening process, several studies were excluded due to unclear reporting of antiemetic use, therefore, limiting the number of included studies. However, two independent reviewers screened each search result for inclusion and resolved discrepancies, minimizing the likelihood of missing potentially eligible studies. Lastly, a formal critical appraisal of the evidence was not conducted due to the generally low quality of evidence provided by the included studies. Most studies had small sample sizes, limited blinding or masking of study participants, and were not adequately powered to detect the effects of the interventions on CINV. Despite these limitations, this scoping review provides valuable insight into the breadth of CIM therapies used for CINV in pediatric patients with cancer and identifies important targets for future research. Conclusion In summary, there is no clear evidence of benefit from the combined use of complementary therapies and pharmacological antiemetics to better control CINV. However, this review adds valuable information to the field of pediatric oncology as CIM is seldom used alone to address CINV, but rather in combination with pharmacological antiemetics. Therefore, investigating the combined effects of these therapies is imperative in working toward better control of CINV. The evidence presented in this review provides support and direction for future research. This future research should aim for high-quality evidence with improved methods and protocols, including longer intervention durations, adherence to pharmacological antiemetic regimens, and larger sample sizes adequately powered to draw definitive conclusions. The most promising avenue presented in this review was ginger, so future research should continue to investigate the herb''s therapeutic effect and safety as well as expand to new CIM interventions not previously used in combination with pharmacological antiemetics. Despite this lack of observed effect, complementary therapies in general have little potential for harm and are relatively low cost and low burden. Therefore, if there is a perceived benefit for some individuals, complementary therapies should be considered. Integrative medicine, with complementary and pharmacological approaches, should be used at the initiation of chemotherapy to increase the likelihood of better control of nausea and vomiting, as well as to better inform and empower patients and families in their treatment options. Pediatric oncology patients deserve the most comfortable and therapeutic experience in their cancer-fighting and healing journeys. With further research and implementation of complementary and integrative medicine, this may be more feasible and readily accessible with chemotherapy delivery. Footnotes The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Funding: The authors disclosed receipt of the following financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article: Research reported in this publication was supported by the National Institute of Arthritis Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under Award K12 AR084223 (Mary O. Whipple). The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. ORCID iDs: Madeleine Kajencki https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6838-6992 Mary O. Whipple https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7073-3224 Supplemental Material: Supplemental material for this article is available online. References Abusaad F., Ali W. (2015). Effect of point 6 acupressure on chemotherapy associated nausea and vomiting among adolescents with cancer. Journal of 