e samper rabanaque lezcano vitoria lus otal zaragoza sepkowitz hafner manoach floris sathianathan clinids sharnprapai suruki corkren etkind mcgarry nardell eid0811 parsonnet ruston schecter schoolnik nejm199406163302402 sudre pfyffer bodmer hom furrer bernasconi telenti strassle s150100050037 suffys degrave oswaldo s0074 tornieporth ptachewich poltoratskaia ravi katapadi dahdouh glatt adamis vachee caillaux pithoud changeon verite simonet tuber tuld vynnycky borgdorff spuy beyers borgdorff helden wachmann agasino borgdorff vynnycky sebek borgdorff deutekom gerritsen ameijden coutinho eisenach gicquel mcadam shinnick rie enarson beyers helden nejm199910143411602 borgdorff sebek veen dessens','Abstract This review briefly summarises the recent achievements in tuberculosis epidemiology associated with the introduction of molecular methods, and considers the implications of these methods for the understanding of occupational tuberculosis transmission. Special attention is paid to the relative contribution of recently transmitted tuberculosis; risk factors for recent transmission; and the occurrence and frequency of exogenous reinfection. There is a need for occupational epidemiological studies, which should combine the methods of "classical" epidemiology with those of molecular epidemiology. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (234.1 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Alland D., Kalkut G. E., Moss A. R., McAdam R. A., Hahn J. A., Bosworth W., Drucker E., Bloom B. R. Transmission of tuberculosis in New York City. An analysis by DNA fingerprinting and conventional epidemiologic methods. N Engl J Med. 1994 Jun 16;330(24):1710–1716. doi: 10.1056/NEJM199406163302403. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bandera A., Gori A., Catozzi L., Degli Esposti A., Marchetti G., Molteni C., Ferrario G., Codecasa L., Penati V., Matteelli A. Molecular epidemiology study of e