a2 turenne winnipeg stellenbosch hrec n09 almeria junta andalucia enr separ boja fastq ena prjeb67987 prjeb67993 prjeb77424 ena prjeb49093 bioproject fastq ena bioprojects prjeb61685 err11275428 err11275429 err11275434 err11275437 err11275501 prjna836783 srr20082812 srr22139594 srr22139590 srr20082804 srr20082810 s0001 s0001 suf1 copyrights gardy cix906 vynnycky khanom hawkey abubakar abascal lago lirola chiner herranz chaoui comas messaoudi jag santanton bouza viedma importations wyllie rathod ebiom merker knoblauch helbling schoch werf kranzer fiebig kroger indra egli cirillo groenheit mengshoel mathys haanpera bottger jajou cirillo tagliani neeling lillebaek spitaleri batignani cabibbe cirillo campino bbac256 bib bbac256 rth mirureader btz771 rajwani shehzad gkh profiler e5090 rabodoarivelo dippenaar grobbelaar gagneux rakotosamimanana grandjean lapierre e84 e92 s2666 krawczyk harmsen vntrplus polyphasic w326 gkq351 allix lesjean oelemann willery savine deutekom roring bifani kurepina vatin fauville skuce locht oppong yea campino mcnerney hibberd viveiros huggett s13073 mazars lesjean banuls gicquel tibayrenc locht minisatellite lesjean savine locht eisenach gicquel mcadam shinnick dippenaar grobbelaar oostvogels cuypers laukens meehan rie e0064621 nilgiriwala rabodoarivelo mandal andrianomanana dingle rodger hoosdally mistry rakotosamimanana grandjean lapierre e0157822 iwai miyazawa kirikae miyoshi castb epidemiologicalanalyses kodmon kodmon ingen bidovec stojkovic cirillo ijtld lirola herranz buenestado inarra bermudez causse galan liro armenteros viudez godoy cabezas lago viedma caprae s0001 s0001 suf1 fastq ena prjeb67987 prjeb67993 prjeb77424 ena prjeb49093 bioproject fastq ena bioprojects prjeb61685 err11275428 err11275429 err11275434 err11275437 err11275501 prjna836783 srr20082812 srr22139594 srr22139590 srr20082804 srr20082810','ABSTRACT The transition from MIRU-VNTR-based epidemiology studies in tuberculosis (TB) to genomic epidemiology has transformed how we track transmission. However, short-read sequencing is poor at analyzing repetitive regions such as the MIRU-VNTR loci. This causes a gap between the new genomic data and the large amount of information stored in historical databases. Long-read sequencing could bridge this knowledge gap by allowing analysis of repetitive regions. However, the feasibility of extracting MIRU-VNTRs from long reads and linking them to historical data has not been evaluated. In our study, an in silico arm, consisting of inference of MIRU patterns from long-read sequences (using MIRUReader program), was compared with an experimental arm, involving standard amplification and fragment sizing. We analyzed overall performance on 39 isolates from South Africa and confirmed reproducibility in a sample enriched with 62 clustered cases from Spain. Finally, we ran 25 consecutive incident cases, demonstrating the feasibility of correctly assigning new clustered/orphan cases by linking data inferred from genomic analysis to MIRU-VNTR databases. Of the 3,024 loci analyzed, only 11 discrepancies (0.36%) were found between the two arms: three attributed to experimental error and eight to misassigned alleles from long-read sequencing. A second round of analysis of these discrepancies resulted in agreement between the experimental and in silico arms in all but one locus. Adjusting the MIRUReader program code allowed us to flag potential in silico misassignments due to suboptimal coverage or unfixed double alleles. Our study indicates that long-read sequencing could help address potential chronological and geographical gaps arising from the transition from molecular to genomic epidemiology of tuberculosis. IMPORTANCE The transition from molecular epidemiology in tuberculosis (TB), based on the analysis of repetitive regions (VNTR-based genotyping), to genomic epidemiology transforms in the precision with which we track transmission. However, short-read sequencing, the most common method for performing genomic analysis, is poor at analyzing repetitive regions. This means that we face a gap between the new genomic data and the large amount of information stored in historical databases, which is also an obstacle to cross-national surveillance involving settings where only molecular data are available. Long-read sequencing could help bridge this knowledge gap by allowing analysis of repetitive regions. Our study demonstrates that MIRU-VNTR patterns can be successfully inferred from long-read sequences, allowing the correct assignment of new cases as clustered/orphan by linking new data extracted from genomic analysis to historical MIRU-VNTR databases. Our data may provide a starting point for bridging the knowledge gap between the molecular and genomic eras in tuberculosis epidemiology. KEYWORDS: tuberculosis, MIRU-VNTR, genomic epidemiology, long-read sequencing INTRODUCTION Mycobacterial interspersed repetitive units-variable number of tandem repeats (MIRU-VNTR) analysis has provided a common analytical language that has been instrumental in advancing our understanding of tuberculosis (TB) transmission over the past decades ( 1 , 2 ). However, its discriminatory power is insufficient for the precision required in many populations in our current scenario, where global migration has increased epidemiological complexity. The higher discriminatory power offered by genomic analysis is well suited to address these new analytical challenges ( 3 – 6 ). However, uncertainties in mapping sequencing reads to repetitive regions make short-read sequencing, which is the most widespread application of genomics in TB epidemiology, less effective for inferring MIRU-VNTR patterns ( 7 , 8 ). This means that the transition to genomics in the analysis of tuberculosis transmission creates knowledge gaps, both chronological and geographical. Once we abandon MIRU-VNTR analysis in favor of genomic analysis, we must assume that it is likely that we will no longer be able to relate new cluster identification data based on genomic analysis to existing knowledge of MIRU-VNTR-based defined clusters, and that it will no longer be possible to make comparisons with countries that have not implemented genomic analysis and only use molecular surveillance. This is a major problem in the current global scenario. Recent developments in long-read sequencing may provide a solution for this knowledge gap, as longer reads can cover entire repetitive regions used for MIRU-VNTR analysis. Several programs have been developed to extract VNTR alleles from long reads ( 9 , 10 ), but shortcomings have been identified in some of them and robust evaluations of their usefulness in different settings are lacking. The aim of our study was to evaluate the inference of MIRU-VNTR patterns from long-read sequencing data on different study subsets to assess its overall performance, reproducibility, and prospective application in real life. Our data may provide a starting point for bridging the knowledge gap between the molecular and genomic eras in tuberculosis epidemiology. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study setting Three subsets of samples were selected for study, belonging to two projects (South Africa and Spain), which had received the ethical approval from the corresponding institutions (Ethical Statement section). Two study samples were retrospective: (i) 39 Mycobacterium tuberculosis clinical isolates from patients routinely diagnosed (years 2011–2017) with rifampicin-resistant tuberculosis in the Western Cape Province (South Africa) ( 11 ), and (ii) 62 isolates representative of seven clusters (identical MIRU-VNTR patterns or tolerating up to one single-locus variant [SLV] if epidemiological links between cases were documented), including more than four cases identified in a population-based MIRU-VNTR molecular epidemiology program in Almeria, Spain (years 2003–2021). The remaining subset was prospective and corresponded to 25 consecutive incident TB cases diagnosed in Almeria between April 13 and August 22, 2023. In addition to the isolates mentioned, we also included six additional isolates from our study in Almería, which are representative of some VNTR loci with ≥10 repeats. Furthermore, 10 long-read sequences, five each of Mycobacterium bovis BCG and M. tuberculosis H37Rv, were included to evaluate the performance in extracting short alleles, 2s and 3s, respectively ( 12 ). MIRU-VNTR analysis (experimental study arm) DNA from the South African subset was purified by phenol-chloroform on heat-inactivated cultured isolates, as previously described ( 11 ), and sent to our laboratory in Madrid for MIRU-VNTR analysis. DNA from the retrospective and prospective samples in Almeria was purified by sodium hydroxide-acetylcysteine after thermosonication inactivation and was extracted using the GXT DNA/RNA Extraction Kit (Bruker, Massachusetts, USA). Twenty-four-locus MIRU-VNTR analysis was performed as described elsewhere ( 13 ). Briefly, PCR was performed to amplify 24 loci in DNA samples using eight triplex PCR reactions. The standard protocol was followed for six triplex reactions using the multiplex PCR kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany). For the remaining two reactions, PuReTaq Ready-To-Go PCR beads (GE Healthcare, Chicago, IL, USA) were used, with the addition of 0.5 µM of each primer and 3% dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO). PCR fragments were sized by capillary electrophoresis (3130 Genetic Analyzer, Applied Biosystems, Waltham, MA, USA). Alleles were assigned using GeneMapper 4.0 (Thermo Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA). In Almeria, the same procedures were followed, and PuReTaq Ready-To-Go PCR beads were also used for any uncertain allele assignations in the first analysis. Long-read nanopore sequencing ( in silico study arm) DNA was purified from Löwenstein-Jensen cultures using the Wizard Genomic DNA Purification Kit (Promega, Wisconsin, USA) for the samples from Almeria, and by phenol-chloroform extraction for the samples from South Africa. Libraries were prepared from purified DNA using the Ligation Sequencing Kit (SQK-LSK109) and the Native Barcoding Kit (EXP-NBD104 and EXP-NBD114) for the retrospective subsets from Almeria and South Africa ( 11 ), and the Rapid Sequencing DNA-PCR Barcoding Kit (SQK-RPB004) for the prospective subset from Almeria (ONT, Oxford, UK), following the manufacturer’s instructions. The final libraries were loaded onto a MinION flow cell (R.9.4.1 FLO-MIN106). An in-house pipeline analysis ( https://github.com/MG-IiSGM/prokaION ) was applied to examine the acquired sequencing data. The workflow of this pipeline included the following steps: (i) preprocessing of the initial fast5 files into the more accessible FASTQ format by basecalling and barcoding using Guppy v.6.4.6, and (ii) quality assessment using NanoFilt v2.8.0 and NanoPlot v1.41.0 to ensure the most reliable and informative data for subsequent analysis. Inference of MIRU-VNTR alleles from long reads After the FASTQ files with quality reads were trimmed, filtered by base quality and fragment length, the MIRUReader tool ( https://github.com/phglab/MIRUReader ) ( 9 ) was run. Briefly, this program takes the input reads in FASTQ or FASTA format and uses the EMBOSS primersearch tool to scan the long reads and identify each of the PCR primer sequences used to analyze each MIRU-VNTR locus. The base pair distance between the primers is then calculated, and the number of repeats (allele value) associated with this distance is determined using a reference allele-calling table ( 12 ). Short-read sequencing and inference of MIRU-VNTR alleles from short reads Libraries were prepared from purified DNA using Nextera XT kit (Illumina) following the manufacturer’s instructions and were run on a MiSeq device (2 × 151 bp). FASTQ files were pre-processed with fastp v0.23.2. This involved quality filtering by applying a Phred score >Q30, a minimum length of 35 base pairs and an additional parameter for adapter detection in paired-end reads. Subsequently, the MIRUReader tool was executed by inputting both FASTQ files, R1 and R2. Lineage identification The lineages were extracted from the sequences of each strain using the annotation of lineage and sublineage markers obtained from TB-Profiler ( 14 ) through an in-house script within prokaION pipeline. RESULTS We evaluated an “ in silico study arm”, based on inferring MIRU-VNTR patterns from long-read sequences and compared it with the standard “experimental study arm”, based on multiplex PCR of the MIRU-VNTR loci and sizing of amplification products to calculate the number of repetitions and assign the corresponding allele values. One hundred twenty-six M. tuberculosis complex isolates were selected and distributed in three study subsets, each offering a different composition of isolates, to (i) test suitability for an initial assessment of performance (convenience sample from South Africa [ 11 ]), (ii) evaluate reproducibility (retrospective sample of clusters including >4 cases from Almeria, Spain), and (iii) run a real-life application on consecutive incident cases (Almerian prospective sample). Initial performance assessment The South African subset consisted of 39 isolates with MIRU-VNTR data available, distributed as follows: 22 clustered isolates (six clusters, two to seven cases each; Table S1) and 17 orphan genotypes (a total of 23 different genotypes to be inferred in the in silico arm of the study). Regarding lineages, they corresponded to L.1, L.2, and L.4 (Table S1). Discrepancies between experimentally assigned allele values and those inferred from long reads were found in 1 out of 936 allele assignments (0.11%) for the 24 loci analyzed per isolate ( Table 1 ; Table S1). TABLE 1. Discrepant alleles (in bold) in the MIRU-VNTR pattern between experimental and in silico (long reads) results across the different data sets MIRU-VNTR b Initial performance subset: South Africa Cluster 3 MIRU-VNTR consensus reference pattern a 2 2 5 1 3 3 2 6 1 3 3 5 3 5 – 4 3 3 – 4 11 4 2 3 Patient 16 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 3 2 6 1 3 3 5 3 5 – 4 3 3 – 4 11 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 3 2 6 1 3 3 5 3 5 – 4 3 3 6 4 11 4 2 3 Reproducibility subset: Almería Cluster 76 MIRU-VNTR consensus reference pattern a 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 5 3 5 3 3 2 3 3 4 2 3 5 2 3 4 2 3 Patient 44 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 5 3 5 3 3 2 3 3 4 2 3 5 2 3 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 5 3 5 3 3 2 3 3 2 2 3 5 2 3 4 2 3 Cluster 113 MIRU-VNTR consensus reference pattern a 2 2 6 1 3 1 2 5 4 4 2 3 4 2 2 2 1 2 3 2 6 4 2 1 Patient 65 Experimental 2 3 6 1 3 1 2 5 4 4 2 3 4 2 2 2 1 2 3 2 6 4 2 1 Long reads 2 2 6 1 3 1 2 7 4 4 2 3 4 2 2 2 1 2 3 2 6 4 2 1 Cluster 833 MIRU-VNTR consensus reference pattern a 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Patient 74 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 4 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 4 2 4 4 2 3 Patient 75 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 4 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 4 2 4 4 2 3 Patient 76 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 4 2 4 4 2 3 Patient 83 Experimental 2 3 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 4 2 4 4 2 3 Patient 85 Experimental 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 5 2 4 4 2 3 Long reads 2 2 5 1 3 2 2 4 1 3 3 3 2 4 2 2 5 2 4 2 4 4 2 3 Prospective subset: Almería Patient 126 Experimental 3 1 6 1 3 2 2 5 7 4 3 2 3 4 2 1 2 4 2 3 6 4 2 3 Long reads 3 1 6 1 3 2 2 5 7 4 3 2 3 4 2 1 2 4 2 2 6 4 2 3 Open in a new tab a The reference MIRU-VNTR pattern of the respective cluster is indicated. b Order of loci in the MIRU-24 code: M2 (0154), M20 (2059), M23 (2531), M24 (2687), M27 (3007), M39 (4348), M4 (0580), M26 (2996), M40 (0802), M10 (0960), M16 (1644), M31 (3192), M42 (0424), M43 (0577), ETRA (2165), M47 (2401), M52 (3690), M53 (4156), QUB-11b (2163b), 1955, QUB-26 (4052), M46 (2347), M48 (2461), M49 (3171). Although MIRUReader inferences were correct even for the loci with ≥10 repetitions (Table S1), we added six new isolates from our database with ≥10 repetitions at other loci, from our study in Almería, to ensure diversity. Specifically, involved locus 3690 (M52) with allele values of 10, 11, and 13 each occurring once; locus 0802 (M40) with an allele value of 10; and locus 4052 (Qub26) with an allelic value of 10. Correct in silico inferences were also obtained for all of them. Additionally, other alleles with special values were analyzed, such as the short alleles 2s and 3s. To do this, we used sequences deposited in different ENA bioprojects of M. bovis BCG (five sequences) and M. tuberculosis H37Rv (five sequences) strains that present these 2s and 3s values at locus 0580 (M4), respectively ( 12 ). Correct in silico inferences of the alleles were also obtained for all of them. Analysis of reproducibility The next step was to evaluate the degree of reproducibility of in silico inference of MIRU-VNTR patterns from long reads. For this, we selected a different subset of samples, consisting exclusively of clustered isolates, to assess how robust MIRU-VNTR pattern extraction was from independent isolates with the same allele values (same MIRU-VNTR pattern). Sixty-two clustered isolates (involved in seven extensive/long-term clusters; 4–13 cases in each cluster; Table S1) were selected from our population-based retrospective analysis in Almeria. Regarding lineages, all of them corresponded to L.4 (Table S1). Only nine discrepancies (0.6%) between the experimental and in silico arms ( Table 1 ; Table S1) were identified in a total of 1,488 loci analyzed. The 672 allele assignments obtained from all 28 isolates belonging to four clusters (four to nine isolates per cluster) were fully concordant. Discrepancies were limited to a few representatives of three clusters, with the majority of discrepancies found in cluster 833 ( Table 1 ; Table S1). Prospective application on incident cases Having observed retrospectively the good performance of MIRU-VNTR pattern inference from long reads (only 10 discrepancies out of 2,424 loci analyzed; 0.41%), the next step was to exploit its real-world potential to investigate whether new incident cases are clustered or not, using our pre-existing historical population-based MIRU-VNTR database as a reference (including 1,867 genotyped isolates from the last 20 years). To this end, we performed long-read sequencing of all 25 consecutive tuberculosis cases diagnosed in Almeria between April 13 and August 22, 2023 (Table S1). Regarding lineages, they corresponded to L.4 and L.6, and one Mycobacterium caprae (Table S1). Inference of the MIRU-VNTR pattern from the long reads allowed 12 cases to be assigned to clusters previously defined in our MIRU-VNTR-based surveillance program and three cases formed a new cluster each ( Table 2 ). Two clusters added two cases, and the others added one case each. More than half of the clusters had five or more members ( Table 2 ); in 6 of the 13 clusters involved, new cases were added in 2022 and 2023, indicating active clusters. One cluster involved only autochthonous patients, whereas the others involved either exclusively migrant patients ( 7 ) or mixed ( 5 ). Finally, one of the MIRU-VNTR inferences correctly assigned a case to a long-term zoonosis involving M. caprae , which alerted us to the current activity of this relevant cluster. Taken together, the data demonstrate the feasibility of linking new data from long-read genomics with historical MIRU-VNTR databases. TABLE 2. Clusters adding new cases in the prospective analysis a Cluster ID Years Nationality composition Previous cluster size + additional cases (long-read sequencing) 015-1 2003–2022 Mixed (Spain, Romania, Bolivia) 16 + 2 2816-1 2019–2022 Migrant (Mali, Senegal, Morocco, Burkina Faso) 9 + 2 076-1 2003–2017 Autochthonous 15 + 1 2778-1 2020 Migrant uninational (Senegal) 1 + 1 1858-1 2014–2018 Migrant uninational (Morocco) 2 + 1 386-1 2005–2022 Migrant uninational (Morocco) 5 + 1 347-1 2006–2019 Mixed (Senegal, Mauritania, Spain) 9 + 1 3113-1 2023 Migrant (Guinea-Bissau, Gambia, Burkina Faso) 3 + 1 493-1 2006–2020 Mixed (Morocco, Mauritania, Spain, Guinea-Bissau) 9 + 1 2739-1 2020 Mixed (Spain, Gambia) 1 + 1 1485-1 ( M. caprae ) 2011–2022 Mixed (Mali, Mauritania, Spain) 5 + 1 3083-1 2022–2023 Migrant (Morocco, Senegal) 2 + 1 1348 2010 Migrant uninational (Morocco) 1 + 1 Open in a new tab a The table includes cluster ID, active years range, patient nationalities, and cluster size. For the remaining 10 samples, the inferred MIRU-VNTR patterns had not been previously recorded in our population-based database, indicating no association with existing clusters and were therefore classified as orphan strains (Table S1). Finally, the MIRU-VNTR patterns obtained by in silico inference were compared with the values obtained by the standard experimental approach. We observed only one discrepancy out of a total number of 600 assignments (0.17%; Table 1 ; Table S1). Analysis of discrepancies between the experimental and in silico arms Resolution of discrepancies In total, we identified 11 allele discrepancies between the experimental and in silico arms ( Table 1 ). Ten of the 11 discrepancies involved isolates that belonged to clusters, whereas the remaining one corresponded to an orphan isolate. For each discrepancy, we performed a second round of analysis by repeating the procedure that was most likely to have caused the error (PCR-based experimental analysis or nanopore sequencing to infer the MIRU-VNTR profile). To determine which analytical arm was the source of the error, we considered the correct allele to be the one found in the majority of the representatives in each cluster. As a result, we were able to assign the error to the experimental arm in two isolates (both at locus M20, patients 65 and 83; Table 1 ) and to the in silico arm in the remaining eight ( Table 1 ). We lacked a reference for the only discrepancy in an orphan case (patient 126) and were unable to assign either arm as the most likely source of the error. Next, for the probable errors in the experimental arm, we performed a new PCR, followed by fragment sizing, and for those in the in silico arm, we applied nanopore sequencing again, ensuring a minimum mean final depth of 20×. After repeating the corresponding procedures, in all but one case (90.9%), we obtained the allele considered to be the correct reference and thus resolved the initial discrepancies. The only exception was an in silico misassignment at locus M26 (2996) for patient 65 ( Table 1 ; Table S1). The orphan case for which we have no reference for the corrected allele was among those repeated experimentally, and the discrepancy with the in silico inference (locus 1955) was also resolved ( Table 1 ). Analysis of the reasons for discrepancies The correct allele values obtained after repeating the analysis on the discrepancies in the experimental arm suggested human error in the first round of analysis. For the remaining eight discrepancies in the in silico arm, the internal outputs of the program were examined to identify a possible cause. In two cases, the incorrectly assigned allele was based solely on a single amplicon product (locus QUB-11b in patient 16 and locus M47 in patient 44; Table 1 ). Another five discrepancies involved the same locus (M52) within the same cluster (833) ( Table 1 ). The experimental analysis assigned the allele 5, whereas the in silico inference was 4. The program output showed that the results were supported by a sufficient number of amplicons (from 7 to 19). However, in all cases, two allele values (4 and 5) coexisted, and the program assigned the allele 4, based on its majority frequency. In four of the five cases, the frequency values of the two alleles were very close, with the majority value <0.6. In the fifth case, the allele frequency of the majority value was 0.74 (patient 85). Finally, in only one discrepancy could we not find an explanation for the misassignment (locus M46 in patient 65; Table 1 ). Alerting to potential errors in allele assignment from long-read sequencing After identifying the possible reasons for the misassignment of certain allele values when inferring the MIRU-VNTR from long-read sequences, we decided to access the original code of the MIRUReader program and forked it to include an alert mechanism for similar cases in the future. The alerts were specifically designed to monitor two situations based on our analysis of discrepancies. First, for loci with suboptimal sequencing depth: when the number of amplicons for any locus is <3, the modified MIRUReader program ( https://github.com/MG-IiSGM/MIRUReader ) now provides an allele value accompanied by a “low-coverage” warning. Second, for loci with intermediate allele frequencies: when the frequency of the dominant allele is <0.6, the program will issue an “unfixed allele” warning (Table S1). The modified MIRUReader was run again on all isolates in the study. Warning alerts were duly issued for all six discrepancies that had previously been analyzed as being the result of either low coverage or unfixed alleles (Table S1). In addition, alerts were issued for a further 14 results, that had not led to discrepancies, because they showed suboptimal coverage (in nine isolates) or unfixed alleles (in five) (Table S1). DISCUSSION When MIRU-VNTR was introduced as an alternative for a more precise identification of tuberculosis clusters, candidates to be part of the same transmission chain, all the information obtained with the previous tools, IS 6110 -RFLP and spoligotyping, was decoupled from the new data ( 15 – 17 ). This resulted in the loss of highly valuable information that could not be communicated with the new data. The situation is similar with the introduction of genomic analysis for a more accurate tracking of TB transmission in the current highly complex epidemiological scenario. The gap between genomic and molecular VNTR data is the result of the limitations of standard short-read sequencing in characterizing the repetitive regions used as loci for MIRU-VNTR studies. It is hoped that a new generation of long-read sequencing, which is also gradually being introduced in the TB context ( 11 , 18 – 20 ), can overcome these limitations, as longer reads can map accurately to these regions and infer the allele values for repetitions at these loci. Regarding laboratory expenses, a complete MIRU-VNTR analysis based on multiplex PCR and sizing in capillary electrophoresis costs around 40 euros, whereas nanopore sequencing, when multiplexing and reusing the flow cell, could cost twice as much but provides the complete genome. Several programs/platforms have been developed that are able to identify repetitive regions from long-read sequencing data and determine the number of repeats in order to assign the corresponding allele values. MIRU-profiler ( 10 ) requires assemblies, which are more prone to errors. Another program, MIRUReader ( 9 ), requires only long reads and appears to lead to a lower proportion of misassignments than MIRU-profiler. Finally, MIRU-VNTRplus ( 12 ) and CASTB ( 21 ) are not locally installed programs but service platforms that do not support high-throughput analysis and are therefore not suitable for systematic analysis. We decided to select MIRUReader instead of MIRU-profiler for our analysis, as it allows the direct exploitation of long reads without requiring assemblies. The overall result of comparing the experimental methodology based on PCR and sizing of the amplified products with in silico inference of allele values from long-read nanopore sequencing by MIRUReader was 11 discrepancies (only 0.36% of all loci analyzed). Eight of these were misassignments attributed to the in silico analysis, and three to experimental errors. We performed the same inference of MIRU-VNTR values, but using short reads instead of long reads (data not shown) and, as expected, due to the mapping errors inherent to short reads, results were clearly inferior (only 1 out of the 2,893 alleles tested was correct). Finally, we analyzed the same complete data set by MIRU-profiler and it wrongly assigned or failed to assign an allele value at 777 of the 3,024 (25.69%). It is true that the underperformance of MIRU-profiler in the current study might be related to some limitations on the nanopore reads used to obtain the assembled data, which are required in this analysis, and not to limitations inherent to the program itself. Nevertheless, the differential performances of the two programs on our data sets, together with the easiness of just requiring the usage of long reads (without the need to assemble), justified our decision of having selected the MIRUReader for our study. A second round of discrepancy analysis revealed agreement between the in silico and experimental study arms. For the discrepancies in the experimental arm, the role of human error in the first round was suggested. The experimental approach is laborious and susceptible to human error because it involves performance of eight multiplex PCRs and assignment of alleles to the 24 corresponding loci, so erroneous results may occasionally occur. International validations of MIRU-VNTR performed in different laboratories have also observed discrepancies in the assignment of the correct MIRU-VNTR pattern from the same test panel ( 22 , 23 ). Regarding the discrepancies in the in silico arm, an in-depth analysis of the bioinformatics results clarified the reasons for the errors in the inference of the allele value in all but one case. These involved either a low sequencing coverage at the locus in question or a double unfixed allele. To alert the user to these two types of potential error when inferring MIRU-VNTR from long reads, we modified the MIRUReader code so that a warning was issued when an allele assignment was supported by <3 amplicons and/or when two allele values coexisted at the same position with a frequency <0.6. Once these alerts are identified, caution should be exercised before accepting that allele as correct; as the MIRUReader program can be executed while the nanopore sequencing is running, we can monitor the run to ensure that it is not stopped until the minimum coverage is achieved, by checking that the warnings of “low coverage” implemented in MIRUReader are not flagged. In all but one of the discrepancies detected in the in silico arm of our study, a second round of nanopore sequencing, ensuring an approximately 20-fold coverage, was sufficient to resolve them. Even after implementing the alert, two of our discrepancies would have passed the overall screening, either because they had good coverage or because the frequency was >0.6 despite the double alleles. Therefore, we must assume a misassignment rate of 0.07% in our study for inference of MIRU-VNTR from long-read sequences. Our study presents a more comprehensive analysis of MIRU-VNTR patterns inferred from long reads compared to previous evaluations ( 9 ) as we included isolates from two different settings, South Africa and Spain. We must acknowledge that our samples are restricted, primarily, to lineages L2 and L4; nevertheless, we have assured a high level of allelic diversity across the different loci (between 2 and 10). In addition to the efficiency of the extraction of different alleles, we also demonstrated the reproducibility of the inference of MIRU-VNTR patterns by including a specific study subset enriched by multiple isolates representative of certain clustered genotypes. The main added value of our study was the evaluation of 25 actual consecutive incident cases over a 4-month period in a population-based study in a complex epidemiological setting with a high proportion of TB cases in migrants (79.7% in 2023). This subset of the study replicated the real-world situation we wanted to address: whether it would be possible to link data obtained from new incident cases to our historical MIRU-VNTR databases once MIRU-VNTR analysis is discontinued in favor of genomic analysis. Our simulation was able to correctly identify all the new cases that deserved special attention because they were part of clusters that had been previously identified in our population and confirmed to be due to ongoing transmission by coupling a thorough epidemiological investigation of the clustered cases. Twelve of the 25 incident cases (48%) were managed according to their epidemiological links to the 10 pre-existing clusters, representing different scenarios of likely transmission. Furthermore, three of these incident cases each formed a new cluster with previous cases. Importantly, these clusters encompassed quite diverse epidemiological scenarios, including migrant and autochthonous cases only, patients from various countries, small clusters involving two to four patients, or large active clusters involving up to 19 patients that have remained active for the last 20 years. Furthermore, accurate MIRU-VNTR extraction correctly assigned the cause of one infection as M. caprae , and the epidemiological investigation was redirected accordingly, as in our region, a long-term zoonotic event with the involvement of different M. caprae ( 24 ) strains has been described. Once matches are established between the new data obtained by nanopore sequencing and the historical VNTR databases, to increase the robustness of the matches found, we would recommend to sequence a few representative archived isolates. We acknowledge that our proposal to link new genomic data to historical MIRU-VNTR data means using long-read sequencing only to analyze incident cases rather than short reads. It is expected that genomic data obtained from new incident cases can be used not only for backward/retrospective extraction of MIRU-VNTR patterns but also for the performance of detailed analyses based on the single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) identified, regarding of relationships between prospective cases and the available genomic data. The decision to base our analysis on long reads does not preclude a more refined analysis based on SNP characterization; a recent multinational study demonstrated that long-read and short-read analyses are equivalent in terms of SNP calling for diagnostic or epidemiological purposes ( 19 ). The extraction of MIRU-VNTR patterns from long-read genomic data represents a significant advance in automated MIRU-VNTR typing of M. tuberculosis and provides a solution to the knowledge gaps in chronological and geographical analysis associated with the discontinuation of MIRU-VNTR analysis once prospective genomic characterization is implemented. We can now be confident that there is a link to historical databases, particularly important in settings such as ours, where cluster identification triggers epidemiological intervention. Our findings could also be made extensive to other pathogens traditionally surveyed by VNTR-based fingerprinting schemes. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This work was supported by the Instituto de Salud Carlos III (PI21/01823 to D.G.V., PI19/00331 to L.P.-L.; a Miguel Servet Contract [CPII20/00001] to L.P.-L.; and a PFIS contract [FI22/00145] to S.B.-S.) and co-financed by European Regional Development Funds of the European Commission: “A way of making Europe” (ERDF) to P.M., D.G.V., and L.P.-L. The authors are grateful to Janet Dawson for editing and proofreading assistance. Conceptualization was primarily carried out by D.G.V. and L.P.-L. Methodology development involved S.B.-S., M.M.-L., A.D., M.H., A.S.-P., A.G.-T., J.A.G.-C., A.B.E.-G., and A.D. The formal analysis was conducted by S.B.-S., M.M.-L., M.H., A.S.-P., C.R.-G., and A.B.E.-G. Resources were managed collectively by D.G.V., L.P.-L., P.M., and R.W. The initial draft of the manuscript was written by D.G.V. and S.B.-S., and corrected and revised by L.P.-L. All authors participated in the subsequent writing, revising, and editing processes. Overall supervision was by D.G.V. and L.P.-L. Contributor Information Laura Pérez-Lago, Email: lperezg00@gmail.com. Darío García de Viedma, Email: dgviedma2@gmail.com. Christine Y. Turenne, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada ETHICAL STATEMENT Samples analyzed from South Africa were from an anonymized pre-existing retrospective collection of clinical isolates, and our analyses led to no clinical intervention; no human data were used in this study. South African data are part of a biobank housed at Stellenbosch University approved under SU-HREC project number N09/11/296. Samples analyzed from Almería were approved by sequential approvals from Junta de Andalucía Ethical Committee (the first one 60/2017 and the latest one Ref EMC/enr, SEPAR 2023, 98/2023; following requirements as published in BOJA number 24 5/2/2020). DATA AVAILABILITY Data supporting the findings of this study (FASTQ files) have been deposited in the European Nucleotide Archive (ENA) at https://www.ebi.ac.uk under project accession numbers PRJEB67987 , PRJEB67993 , and PRJEB77424 . Data from the South African subset have been deposited in the ENA PRJEB49093 bioproject. Additionally, the FASTQ sequences used to validate the in silico performance in inferring short alleles, 2s and 3s, were obtained from the ENA bioprojects PRJEB61685 , corresponding to Mycobacterium bovis BCG isolates ( ERR11275428 , ERR11275429 , ERR11275434 , ERR11275437 , and ERR11275501 ) and PRJNA836783 , referencing Mycobacterium tuberculosis H37Rv isolates ( SRR20082812 , SRR22139594 , SRR22139590 , SRR20082804 , and SRR20082810 ). SUPPLEMENTAL MATERIAL The following material is available online at https://doi.org/10.1128/jcm.00741-24 . Table S1. jcm.00741-24-s0001.xlsx. Details of each patient in the dataset, showing their lineages and respective MIRU-VNTR patterns with allele assignments by the two approaches. jcm.00741-24-s0001.xlsx (54.3KB, xlsx) DOI: 10.1128/jcm.00741-2