 communication following newborn screening for cystic fibrosis. Health Commun. 2004;16(2):197–205. doi: 10.1207/S15327027HC1602_4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] La Pean A, Farrell MH. Initially misleading communication of carrier results after newborn genetic screening. Pediatrics. 2005;116(6):1499–1505. doi: 10.1542/peds.2005-0449. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Farrell MH, La Pean A, Ladouceur L. Content of communication by pediatric residents after newborn genetic screening. Pediatrics. 2005;116(6):1492–1498. doi: 10.1542/peds.2004-2611. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ciske DJ, Haavisto A, Laxova A, Rock LZ, Farrell PM. Genetic counseling and neonatal screening for cystic fibrosis: an assessment of the communication process. Pediatrics. 2001;107(4):699–705. doi: 10.1542/peds.107.4.699. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Stratakis CA, Cavuto NJ, Nelson D, Rennert OM. Molecular genetics in pediatric training: how much do we really know? Md Med J. 1995;44(3):210–213. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Berg JM, Gilderdale S, Way J. On telling parents of a diagnosis of mongolism. Br J Psychiatry. 1969;115(527):1195–1196. doi: 10.1192/bjp.115.527.1195. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Makoul G. Essential elements of communication in medical encounters: the Kalamazoo consensus statement. Acad Med. 2001;76(4):390–393. doi: 10.1097/00001888-200104000-00021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rosen A, Wallenstein S, McGovern MM. Attitudes of pediatric residents toward ethical issues associated with genetic testing in children. Pediatrics. 2002;110(2 Pt 1):360–363. doi: 10.1542/peds.110.2.360. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cooley WC, McAllister JW. Building medical homes: improvement strategies in primary care for children with special health care needs. Pediatrics. 2004;113(5 Suppl):1499–1506. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Wilson GN, Cooley WC. Preventive Health Care for Children with Genetic Conditions: Providing a Primary Care Medical 