eted COVID‐19 Vaccination Is More Equitable.” [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 35. Zeng S. et al., “Association of Zip Code Vaccination Rate with COVID‐19 Mortality in Chicago, Illinois,” JAMA Network Open 5, no. 5 (2022): doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2022.14753. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. McClung et al., “The Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices’ Ethical Principles for Allocating Initial Supplies of COVID‐19 Vaccine,” 1782. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 37. “CDC''s Social Vulnerability Index (SVI),” Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, October 15, 2020, at https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/placeandhealth/svi/index.html . 38. Dooling et al., “The Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices’ Updated Interim Recommendation for Allocation of COVID‐19 Vaccine—United States, December 2020,” 1659. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 39. Press V. G., Huisingh‐Scheetz M., and Arora V. M., “Inequities in Technology Contribute to Disparities in COVID‐19 Vaccine Distribution,” JAMA Health Forum 2, no. 3 (2021): doi: 10.1001/jamahealthforum.2021.0264. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. White D. B. and Angus D. C., “A Proposed Lottery System to Allocate Scarce COVID‐19 Medications: Promoting Fairness and Generating Knowledge,” Journal of the American Medical Association 324, no. 4 (2020): 329–30. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Kerr C. C. et al., “Covasim: An Agent‐Based Model of COVID‐19 Dynamics and Interventions,” PLOS Computational Biology 17, no. 7 (2021): doi: 10.1371/journal.pcbi.1009149. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Oliver S. E., “Use of the Janssen (Johnson & Johnson) COVID‐19 Vaccine: Updated Interim Recommendations from the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices—United States, December 2021,” Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report 71, (2022): 90–95; M. Wallace, “The Advisory Committee on Immunizati